1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Bugis sailors saw — a compass.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:08,000
The Bugis of South Sulawesi, Indonesia,
are famous traders and sailors.

3
00:00:13,500 --> 00:00:19,000
Crews told anthropologist Gene Ammarell
which star rises or sets over which
island.

4
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:34,500
Their most-used guides: Alpha Centauri
and Hadar, side by side — bintoéng
balué, the Widow.

5
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:40,500
From Balobaloang, she marks the way to
Bima. Sailors set course by her.

6
00:00:40,500 --> 00:00:48,000
Beside her, bola képpang: one post is
shorter, so the house leans.

7
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:54,500
And north? At 5° S, the Pole Star is
always below the horizon.

8
00:00:54,500 --> 00:01:00,500
North is marked by bintoéng kappala'é —
like their own wooden sailboats.

9
00:01:00,500 --> 00:01:07,000
Bintoéng timoro'. Seen from Sulawesi, it
rises almost due east.

10
00:01:07,000 --> 00:01:14,000
North of the equator, the Big Dipper
hangs handle down — the Bugis ship.

11
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,000
The Mandar people see a fishing net
around the Dipper.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

